      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a16.7.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g16.7.7', 'g16.7.11', 'g16.7.26'],
          lemma: "συμφέρει ὑμῖν ἵνα ἐγὼ ἀπέλθω",
          title: "“It is to your advantage that I go away”",
          body: "It is a staggering thing to say to grieving friends: *my leaving is for your good.* How could the disciples possibly be better off without the physical presence of Jesus? The answer is the whole logic of Pentecost. As long as Jesus is present in the body, he is present in *one* place at a time — with these men in this room, but not with the church in every land and age. His going — through the cross, resurrection, and ascension — is the doorway to the Spirit’s coming, and the Spirit is Christ present *everywhere at once* and *within* his people, not merely beside them. So the exchange is pure gain: the localized, external presence gives way to the universal, internal one. This is why he can call his departure an *advantage.* The disciples are not being downgraded from Jesus to a lesser consolation prize; they are being brought from Christ-with-them to Christ-in-them, from a presence they could lose to one that abides forever (14:16). What feels like the end of everything is in fact the arrangement by which he will never leave.",
        },
      ],
